# ─── Secrets: match on substance, not on an exact filename ────────────────────
# Two failure modes this closes, both observed on 2026-07-27:
#
#  1. Drive/OneDrive sync writes shadow copies with the sync suffix BEFORE the
#     extension — ".env (1).local", "runner (1).env.s1". Every extension-anchored
#     rule (".env.local", "*.env") misses those. ".env (1).local" reached `git add -A`
#     staged in cryptacount-api and was caught only by reading the staged file list
#     before pushing. cryptachain had seven such files staging-eligible at once.
#  2. Rules that enumerate go stale. The first draft of THIS block enumerated
#     " (1)", " (2)", " (3)" and a probe file named ".env (9).local" sailed through
#     it — the same defect it was written to fix. Match the shape, never the list.
#
# Order matters: last match wins, so the re-includes MUST stay below the excludes.
# Verify changes with brand-new probe files, not with tracked ones: `git check-ignore`
# reports "not ignored" for anything already in the index, so testing against tracked
# files is vacuous and will pass no matter what these rules say.

# Sync shadow copies — any parenthesised suffix, any number. Nothing with one is
# ever intentional.
*.orig
*.bak

# Secret-bearing files by substance.
.env*
*.env
*.env.*
*secret*
*credential*
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
*.key
*.jks
*keystore*
*service-account*.json
*serviceaccount*.json
*.kubeconfig

# …but never swallow source code, templates or docs. Without these, a new file
# named CredentialsManager.java or rotate-secret.sh is silently untracked — a
# failure that stays invisible until someone clones and the file is simply gone.
# Deliberately NOT re-included: *.yml/*.yaml (k8s Secret manifests, application-*.yml)
# and *.json (service-account keys, audit dumps) genuinely do carry secrets.
!*.java
!*.kt
!*.ts
!*.tsx
!*.js
!*.jsx
!*.mjs
!*.cjs
!*.go
!*.py
!*.rs
!*.cs
!*.sql
!*.md
!*.sh
!*.ps1
!*.bat
!*.http
!*.example
!*.sample
!*.template
!*.dist
!*.d.ts

# Sync shadow copies, LAST so they beat every re-include above: "Foo (1).java" and
# "config (2).yml" are never intentional, whatever the extension.
* (*)*
* - Copy*
* copy*
